ter is the default value if no ini-file option exists but is queried.\nS14. The `name` parameter is the name of the ini-variable.\nS15. `addini` takes four parameters: `name`, `help`, `type`, and `default`.\nS16. The `type` parameter specifies the type of the variable, with possible values: 'string', 'bool', 'args', 'linelist', 'paths', and 'pathlist'.\nS17. Using the current working directory to resolve 'paths' and 'pathlist' in the absence of an ini-file was added in version 8.1.\nS18. `default` is the default value if no ini-file option exists but is queried.\nS19. The value of ini-variables can be retrieved via a call to `config.getini(name) <pytest.Config.getini>`.\nS20. `addini` stores the variable's details in `_inidict` and adds the name to `_ininames`.\nS21. `addini` is a function in `_pytest/config/argparsing.py`.\nS22. For 'paths' and 'pathlist' types, they are considered relative to the ini-file.\nS23. `name` is the name of the ini-variable.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in this format:\nS1: supported\nS2: contradicted -- <evidence, at most 12 words>\nS3: unresolved",
